# /etc/tmux.conf — system-wide tmux defaults for the terminal-lobby devvm.
# Installed by scripts/deploy.sh from devvm/tmux.conf.system.
#
# Provenance (Task 1.14 truecolor audit, 2026-07-11): ttyd attaches tmux
# clients with TERM=xterm-256color, which terminfo does not mark RGB-capable,
# so tmux down-converted 24-bit SGR to the 256-colour cube (a 77-colour test
# ramp rendered as ~12 hard bands in the lobby) and no user's ~/.tmux.conf
# enabled RGB either. The lobby's xterm.js client renders 24-bit colour
# natively — tell tmux so. Matches any xterm-compatible attach, including
# the read-only ttyd-ro view and SSH clients with an xterm* TERM.
#
# This file loads BEFORE per-user ~/.tmux.conf and -a APPENDS to the option,
# so users can still override or extend it. Already-running tmux servers
# pick it up at their next server start (tmux reads config when the server
# spawns, not per client).
set -as terminal-features 'xterm*:RGB'

# Provenance ([links] wrapped-URL fix, 2026-07-12): tmux 3.4 STRIPS OSC 8
# hyperlinks from output unless the attaching client's termfeatures include
# 'hyperlinks' (TERM=xterm-256color terminfo doesn't advertise it). The
# lobby's xterm.js handles OSC 8 natively (linkHandler in
# frontend/index.html) and the escape carries the FULL URI — immune to
# visual wrapping — so let it through for xterm-compatible attaches
# (rg --hyperlink, eza, gh & friends; Claude Code does not emit OSC 8).
# Same semantics as the RGB line: applies at each tmux server's next start.
set -as terminal-features 'xterm*:hyperlinks'

# Provenance (desktop smooth-wheel, 2026-07-14, terminal.viktorbarzin.me):
# tmux's DEFAULT copy-mode WheelUpPane/WheelDownPane scroll 5 lines per wheel
# report (`send -X -N5`). The web terminal's frontend now de-damps the
# desktop trackpad and emits many proportional ONE-ROW line-wheels per
# gesture (gestures.wheelSmooth, frontend/index.html) — but each still hits
# the -N5 binding, so copy-mode history scroll comes out 5× too fast and in
# 5-line steps (measured). Dropping it to -N1 makes ONE emitted row scroll
# ONE line, so the frontend's pacing maps 1:1 to content — smooth, native,
# fine-grained history scrolling. tmux's DEFAULT loads before this file and
# no user ~/.tmux.conf rebinds these copy-mode keys, so -N1 wins.
#
# SCOPED TO wizard (Viktor's decision, 2026-07-14): this file is loaded by
# EVERY user's tmux server (it is the box-wide /etc/tmux.conf), and a bare
# rebind here would change copy-mode wheel scroll for EVERY tmux user incl.
# their direct SSH/console — bob and anyone else — silently. Viktor asked to
# keep the finer 1-line scroll to his own account only. The if-shell test
# runs at each server's start; `id -un` is that server's OS user (the web
# terminal and SSH share the SAME per-user tmux server), so wizard's server
# gets -N1 (web + his SSH), every other user keeps tmux's default -N5,
# untouched. Mouse-tracking panes (Claude Code, less --mouse) never run this
# binding regardless (tmux forwards the wheel to the inner app). Applies only
# to tmux servers STARTED AFTER this lands — an already-running server keeps
# its current behavior until its next start (kill-server / reboot), so a live
# session needs a restart to pick it up. Braces + if-shell need tmux ≥3.0
# (box is 3.4). select-pane dropped: redundant inside copy-mode, and it kept
# the block brace-clean.
if-shell '[ "$(id -un)" = wizard ]' {
    bind -T copy-mode-vi WheelUpPane   send-keys -X -N1 scroll-up
    bind -T copy-mode-vi WheelDownPane send-keys -X -N1 scroll-down
    bind -T copy-mode    WheelUpPane   send-keys -X -N1 scroll-up
    bind -T copy-mode    WheelDownPane send-keys -X -N1 scroll-down
}
